Q: What is the strict aliasing rule? A: <p>As addendum to what <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/98650/what-is-the-strict-aliasing-rule/99010#99010">Doug T. already wrote</a>, here
is a simple test case which probably triggers it with GCC:</p>
<h3>File <em>check.c</em></h3>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

void check(short *h,long *k)
{
    *h=5;
    *k=6;
    if (*h == 5)
        printf(&quot;strict aliasing problem\n&quot;);
}

int main(void)
{
    long      k[1];
    check((short *)k,k);
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Compile with <code>gcc</code> <code>-O2</code> <code>-o</code> <code>check check.c</code>.</p>
<p>Usually (with most GCC versions I tried) this outputs &quot;strict aliasing problem&quot;, because the compiler assumes that &quot;h&quot; cannot be the same address as &quot;k&quot; in the &quot;check&quot; function. Because of that the compiler optimizes the <code>if (*h == 5)</code> away and always calls the printf.</p>
<p>For those who are interested here is the x64 assembler code, produced by GCC 4.6.3, running on ubuntu 12.04.2 for x64:</p>
<pre><code>movw    $5, (%rdi)
movq    $6, (%rsi)
movl    $.LC0, %edi
jmp    puts
</code></pre>
<p>So the <em>if</em> condition is completely gone from the assembler code.</p>

Q: Some questions about ARM/ARM64 write combine(NORMAL_NC) memory attribute read/write and memory barriers A: <p>The <code>dsb</code> barrier instructions don't manipulate cache at all.  Generally, the purpose of these barriers is to wait for the effects of previous instructions to become visible to other observers (typically other cores); but for instance, to make a memory write visible, it suffices to ensure it is written to a coherent cache.  It doesn't need to be flushed out of that cache.</p>
<p>Instead, actual data cache management is handled by the <code>dc</code> family of instructions (respectively <code>ic</code> for instruction cache).</p>
<p>Now, these instructions don't let you directly say &quot;write out to L3 cache&quot;, &quot;write out to main memory&quot;, etc.  Instead, the ARM64 architecture specification uses an abstract cache hierarchy, defining conceptual &quot;points&quot; at which certain behavior is guaranteed.  For instance, there is a &quot;point of coherency&quot; at which all agents that access memory (including other cores, DMA hardware, etc) will always see the same contents; a &quot;point of unification&quot; at which CPU data and instruction fetches see the same contents; a &quot;point of persistence&quot; at which contents are preserved across power failure; and so on.</p>
<p>Each hardware system would map these conceptual points to its actual cache hierarchy.  For instance, a system where instruction and data cache are separate at L1 but unified at L2 would define the &quot;point of unification&quot; to be the L2 cache.  A system where the L3 cache snoops the bus for DMA operations might define the &quot;point of coherency&quot; to be the L3 cache; a system which does not might define it as main memory.  A system with non-volatile memory could define &quot;point of persistence&quot; accordingly; and so on.</p>
<p>To know for sure which &quot;point&quot; corresponds to DDR RAM on your hardware, you'd have to consult the documentation for the actual hardware you are using.  However, there's a pretty good chance that &quot;point of coherency&quot; is what you want.  (ARMv9.5 and above defines an optional &quot;point of physical storage&quot; that's an even closer match, but unless your system is very recent, you probably don't have that.)</p>
<p>The <code>dc</code> instructions operate on individual cache lines rather than trying to flush or invalidate the entire cache at once, which would be extremely costly.  This can be specified in various ways; what you most likely want would be virtual address, <code>va</code>.</p>
<p>Putting this all together, what you want between your read and write is <code>dc civac</code> : Data Cache, Clean and Invalidate by Virtual Address to point of Coherency.  &quot;Clean&quot; causes the contents of the cache line to be written out; &quot;Invalidate&quot; marks its contents as invalid so that a future read will force the line to be refilled.</p>
<p>One other wrinkle is that <code>dc</code> does not necessarily execute the cache operation immediately; rather, it may just enqueue it for execution in the background.  To wait for outstanding cache operations to complete, you need a <code>dsb</code> instruction (this functionality of <code>dsb</code> is somewhat separate from its other barrier functions).</p>
<p>So putting this all together, your code could look like the following:</p>
<pre><code>unsigned long test(volatile unsigned long *vaddr, unsigned long val)
{
    *vaddr = val;
    __asm__ volatile (&quot;dc civac, %0 \n\t&quot;
                      &quot;dsb sy \n\t&quot;
                      : : &quot;r&quot; (vaddr) :&quot;memory&quot;);
    return *vaddr;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note: The architecture allows the OS kernel (running at exception level EL1 or higher) to restrict the use of <code>dc</code> by unprivileged code (EL0), because constant cache flushing might slow down other processes and become a denial of service.  So if you're not writing kernel or bare-metal code, you might need to check whether your kernel allows this.</p>
<p>If it doesn't, the <code>dc</code> instruction will trap to the OS instead of actually manipulating the cache.  What happens then is up to the OS.  It might simply terminate your program (e.g. a SIGSEGV / SIGBUS / SIGILL on a Unix-like system).  Or, it could fully or partially emulate the effect of the <code>dc</code>, or just return to your program's next instruction without doing anything at all.  So at EL0, just seeing that your program runs without crashing does not necessarily prove that the desired cache operations actually happened; again, you'll have to consult the documentation or source code of your OS.</p>
<p>Alternatively, your kernel might provide a system call to request cache operations, rather than executing <code>dc</code> directly.</p>

Q: $table.bootstrapTable is not a function A: <p>see this section at the beginning of the module:</p>

<pre><code>try {
bootstrapVersion = parseInt($.fn.dropdown.Constructor.VERSION, 10);
</code></pre>

<p>}
catch (e) {}</p>

<p>parseInt can return NaN so the ideal is to do (after try):</p>

<pre><code>if (bootstrapVersion != 3 &amp;&amp; bootstrapVersion != 4)
bootstrapVersion = 3;
</code></pre>
